It's a short one this week, but don't be fooled, there are four entire universes of topics to explore nestled in these few words...

As I've shared before, the vision for Vivarium is a system for investigating, testing, and implementing "real" artificial or machine intelligence, and is based on four pillars: agents, languages, tools, and infrastructure.

By "real", I mean not a parlor trick, not something that humans just anthropomorphize into seeming intelligence, but actual processes that can perceive, learn, process, and produce new information.

Here are some things to explore along these pillar dimensions:

1. **Agents:** There was an interesting article in Quanta magazine last week, [_"The Polyglot Neuroscientist Resolving How the Brain Parses Language"_](https://www.quantamagazine.org/the-polyglot-neuroscientist-resolving-how-the-brain-parses-language-20251205/). A moments reflection should be sufficient to establish that thoughts are not the same as the languages we use to express them, so this research is extremely relevant in the age of the "LLM Industrial Complex". I reached out to Professor Fedorenko and she kindly suggested a paper to review and another researcher's work to investigate, so I wanted to share those:
    * [_"Dissociating language and thought in large language models"_](https://www.cell.com/trends/cognitive-sciences/abstract/S1364-6613(24)00027-5);
    * [Professor Joshua Tenenbaum](https://scholar.google.com/citations?hl=en&user=rRJ9wTJMUB8C)
    * [_"How to Grow a Mind: Statistics, Structure, and Abstraction"_](https://www.science.org/doi/abs/10.1126/science.1192788)
    * [_"A Global Geometric Framework for Nonlinear Dimensionality Reduction"_](https://www.science.org/doi/abs/10.1126/science.290.5500.2319)
1. **Languages:** I've written previously about [why language is important for Vivarium](/blog/2025-11-08-part-2-the-languages/), and about [some of the very cool things languages can do](/blog/2025-11-23-what-can-languages-do/). This week, I also wrote about more specifically what a [Component Language Interface](/blog/component-language-interface/) would look like. You're probably familiar with the now almost ubiquitous "Application Programming Interface" and the evolution of this to generally mean a remote system being accessible via certain network endpoints. The "Component Language Interface" is a generalization of these ideas using the full power of a language as the interface between components.
1. **Tools:** One of the most important tools for building Vivarium is the [Rubinius language platform](https://rubinius.com). There's a ton of work to do to bring up the new end-to-end compiler based entirely on the LLVM tool chain (instead of hodge-podge of different pieces), so I did not want to waste any time on the legacy components if avoidable, but I decided it would be helpful to have the existing Rubinius code working today. Unfortunately, I ran into a nasty, obscure concurrency issue using the compare-and-swap primitive for the expanded header that the C-API uses to keep the garbage collector sane. More on that below. But the good news is that having the current Rubinius system running while the new compiler is built out enables other experiments, like starting to build out the Python C-API.
1. **Infrastructure:** This one is not about Vivarium _per se_ but rather about news last week of a new project out there. [Evan Phoenix](https://github.com/evanphx), the creator of [Rubinius](https://github.com/rubinius/rubinius) and [his team](https://miren.dev) have released a developer preview of [Miren](). There's a page explaining [how Miren is different](https://miren.dev/compare) than some of the other infrastructure that you may be familiar with. This is relevant to Vivarium for a couple reasons. I wrote last week about [some infrastructure ideas](/newsletters/4-saving-an-afternoon-in-the-library/#computational-infrastructure-versus-infrastructure-as-code) that I think are important for building a system like Vivarium, and more generally, I think we do not yet see enough innovation around infrastructure and we will need that as [more exotic hardware concepts](https://extropic.ai) start to become more widely available.

I'll be writing more about Professor Tenenbaum's work in the future as I dig into more of those papers.

